\section{Put Rates in the Program Graph}

In synchronous dataflow (SDF), every actor firing consumes and produces fixed integer numbers of tokens on each port. ``Synchronous'' here concerns statically related token counts, not necessarily a global wall-clock tick. Balance equations seek positive repetition counts \(q\) such that, for each channel from \(A\) to \(B\), \(q_A p=q_B c\), where \(p\) is production and \(c\) consumption \cite{lee1987sdf}.

A solution gives how many times actors fire in one graph iteration; it does not by itself provide a legal order. A periodic schedule must also avoid consuming unavailable tokens. Initial tokens on feedback edges can make a cycle schedulable, and schedule choice affects peak buffer occupancy while preserving token sequences for deterministic actors.

\section{Solve and Schedule a Two-Actor Graph}

Use an SDF declaration in which bracketed numbers are per-firing rates:

\begin{verbatim}
actor Split  in x[2]  out y[3]
actor Sum3   in y[3]  out z[1]
channel Split.y -> Sum3.y initial 0
\end{verbatim}

The balance equation is \(3q_{\text{Split}}=3q_{\text{Sum3}}\), so the minimal repetition vector is \((1,1)\). One iteration may schedule \(\texttt{Split; Sum3}\), consumes two input tokens, and produces one output token. If \texttt{Sum3} were declared to consume one token, the minimal vector would be \((1,3)\). \Cref{fig:visual-356} separates rates from the chosen sequential order.

