\section{Construct a Periodic Admissible Schedule}

\begin{verbatim}
q := smallest_positive_integer_solution(balance_equations)
remaining := q
tokens := initial_tokens
while some remaining actor count is nonzero:
    choose actor a with remaining[a] > 0
        and enough tokens on every input
    if no such actor exists: reject this schedule search
    fire_symbolically(a, tokens)
    remaining[a] := remaining[a] - 1
emit periodic_schedule_and_peak_fifo_sizes()
\end{verbatim}

Failure of one greedy choice is not proof that no admissible schedule exists; a complete scheduler must backtrack or use a method with an appropriate completeness argument. Inconsistent balance equations, by contrast, rule out a bounded periodic steady state with the declared rates.

\section{Compile Rates as Verified Metadata}

An SDF IR contains actor nodes, typed FIFO edges, fixed consumption and production rates, initial tokens as explicit state, actor effects, repetition-vector and schedule annotations, and provenance to port declarations. Clocks may record deployment periods, but must not be conflated with SDF token rates. Compilation can produce a looped static schedule, preallocated circular buffers, multicore tasks, or statically sized hardware FIFOs.

Legal optimizations include schedule compaction, actor fusion, vectorization, and buffer sharing when they preserve FIFO token order, rates, initial state, and observable effects. Semantic-preservation risks include a wrong repetition vector that causes drift, an omitted initial token that deadlocks, and buffer sharing across overlapping live ranges. Preserve actor-level numeric semantics too: fusing a reduction must not silently reassociate floating-point operations.

\section{Show Why Balance Is Not Readiness}

Add a feedback edge to the two actors. Let \texttt{Split} consume one feedback token per firing as well as its two external inputs, and let \texttt{Sum3} send one token back per firing as well as sending its external output. Both internal edges remain balanced at one firing per actor. With zero initial tokens on both edges, however, neither actor can start even when external input is available in abundance. The scheduler is not missing an ordering trick; the specified graph has no first firing.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
Schedule prefix & Forward tokens & Feedback tokens \\
\hline
Empty, no initial feedback & 0 & 0 \\
Empty, with initial feedback & 0 & 1 \\
\texttt{Split} & 3 & 0 \\
\texttt{Split; Sum3} & 0 & 1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The initial feedback token is program data, not synthetic permission to run. A graph that starts with a zero-valued token can differ from one starting with a one-valued token, despite identical liveness. The table records only occupancy. A separate functional test must propagate the actual token value through the two actor implementations.

\Cref{fig:visual-356-a} shows why the balance equations and the startup test use different facts about the same graph.

