\begin{verbatim}
certify_period(graph, period):
    count := graph.initial_occupancy.copy()
    peak := count.copy()
    for actor in period:
        for edge in actor.inputs:
            require count[edge] >= actor.consume[edge]
            count[edge] -= actor.consume[edge]
        for edge in actor.outputs:
            count[edge] += actor.produce[edge]
            peak[edge] := max(peak[edge], count[edge])
    require count == graph.initial_occupancy
    return peak
\end{verbatim}

Assume that external input and output are available on demand and account for them separately. For the two seeded firings the capacities are three forward and one backward. The same certificate does not automatically apply to a schedule that overlaps actors or reserves all output slots before releasing its own input slots. For such an implementation, simulate the actual reservation and release order rather than counting only at firing boundaries. A replay that starts from the empty, unseeded row should fail on its first firing regardless of which actor is tried.

\paragraph{Keep the checker independent of the scheduler.}
Persist the period as actor IDs, not only as the repetition vector.
A checker can recalculate consumption and production from the graph and
compare both firing counts and peak occupancy against the compiler's
certificate. Prefix readiness alone does not prove that all required
actors fired: the empty schedule passes an occupancy-return test.
